{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; rg -n 'Supplementary|References|bibliography|mark|appendix|clearpage' paper.tex iclr2026_conference.sty .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/*; cat check_paper_locally.py; cat compile_paper_locally.sh; command -v apply_patch; command -v pdftotext; ls /tmp; cat paper_evidence_audit_feedback.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; rg -n 'Supplementary|References|bibliography|mark|appendix|clearpage' paper.tex iclr2026_conference.sty .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/*; cat check_paper_locally.py; cat compile_paper_locally.sh; command -v apply_patch; command -v pdftotext; ls /tmp; cat paper_evidence_audit_feedback.json\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: scientific-evidence\ndescription: |\n  Use when: you start writing a scientific paper.\n  Outputs: a repo-local `scientific-evidence/` bundle generated by the skill script, plus the conventions, schemas, and verification report needed for downstream evidence tracking and auditing.\n---\n\n# scientific-evidence\n\nUse this skill to define or materialize a generic evidence contract for scientific artifacts.\n\nAlways use this skill during paper writing whenever the agent adds quantitative content: manuscript numbers, table entries, figure inputs, derived statistics, or plotting/calculation scripts.\n\n## What this skill is for\n\nThe point of this skill is simple: an agent should not be able to drop important numbers, figures, or derived outputs into a paper without leaving a machine-checkable trail back to real artifacts.\n\nThis skill gives the agent one fixed place and one fixed grammar for that trail:\n- inline `@evidence[[...]]` refs in text-like files\n- `.prov.json` sidecars for generated artifacts\n- a central `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl` for optional structured rollups\n\nIt is deliberately generic. The base contract works even when the workflow spans Python, bash, LaTeX, Markdown, JSON, logs, or other file formats.\n\n## Default workflow\n\n1. Read the current paper/results layout, but do not invent a new evidence layout.\n2. Use the fixed directory structure rooted at `scientific-evidence/`.\n3. Run the skill script first instead of inventing schemas by hand:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir <workspace-root>\n```\n4. For every quantitative paper-writing action, use this skill contract:\n- adding a number to `paper.tex`\n- filling a table cell\n- writing a plotting script\n- writing a calculation/statistics script\n- generating a figure, table, or derived JSON file\n5. Inside plotting or calculation scripts, do not hardcode experiment numbers that should come from artifacts. Read them from files, then leave the corresponding `@evidence[[...]]` refs near the read/derivation logic.\n6. Add inline evidence refs in text-like artifacts and `.prov.json` sidecars for generated outputs.\n7. Run the verifier after the agent writes claims, scripts, figures, or sidecars:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir <workspace-root>\n```\n8. Inspect `scientific-evidence/verification-report.json` and fail hard on dangling links.\n9. Treat the generated markdown spec and JSON Schemas as canonical for downstream agents.\nKeep the verifier behavior explicit:\n- `resolved`: target artifact exists and the verifier can check the locator semantics it understands.\n- `anchored`: target artifact exists, but the locator stays opaque or only hash/path checks were possible.\n- `dangling`: the target or manifest link does not resolve.\n\n## How this is used to verify agent work\n\nVerification works by checking whether the agent left a connected evidence trail.\n\nAt a minimum, a downstream verifier should be able to:\n- scan manuscript text, scripts, and notes for `@evidence[[...]]` refs\n- read `.prov.json` sidecars for figures, tables, and other generated outputs\n- resolve those refs against real files or stable ids\n- classify each link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`\n- fail when a generated artifact has no input trail or when a claimed source does not exist\n\nThis does not prove every scientific claim automatically. It does prove whether the agent left enough structure for an auditor or stricter verifier to replay and inspect the work instead of trusting free-form prose.\n\nA core policy for paper writing is: do not hardcode result numbers inside plotting or calculation scripts when those values should be read from real artifacts. Read from files, derive from those inputs, and attach `@evidence[[...]]` refs that point back to the inputs.\n\n## Fixed directory structure\n\n```text\nscientific-evidence/\n  spec.md\n  bundle.manifest.json\n  ledger.jsonl\n  schemas/\n    evidence-reference.schema.json\n    artifact-manifest.schema.json\n    ledger-entry.schema.json\n    verification-report.schema.json\n```\n\n## Grammar summary\n\n- Evidence ref token: `@evidence[[TARGET]]` or `@evidence[[TARGET#LOCATOR]]`\n- `TARGET` may be a relative path, absolute path, URI, or stable identifier.\n- `LOCATOR` is opaque unless a verifier adapter knows how to interpret it.\n- Generated artifact sidecar name: `<artifact>.prov.json`\n- Central ledger file: `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Schema set\n\nGenerate the canonical schema bundle with the script. The bundle includes:\n- `scientific-evidence/spec.md`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/evidence-reference.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/artifact-manifest.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/ledger-entry.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/verification-report.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/bundle.manifest.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Examples\n\nPython:\n```python\n# Read the metric from disk; do not hardcode 96.7 here.\n# @evidence[[results/metrics.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nimport json\npayload = json.loads(open(\"results/metrics.json\", encoding=\"utf-8\").read())\nvalue = payload[\"mnist\"][\"accuracy_mean\"]\n```\n\nBash:\n```bash\n# @evidence[[results/run_summary.csv#row(dataset=mnist,variant=proposed).accuracy]]\npython scripts/summarize_results.py --input results/run_summary.csv\n```\n\nLaTeX:\n```tex\nAccuracy reached 96.7\\%. % @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\n```\n\nMarkdown:\n```md\nMain result: 96.7%. <!-- @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]] -->\n```\n\nR:\n```r\n# @evidence[[results/summary.tsv#humanoid.edppo.mean]]\nsummary <- read.delim(\"results/summary.tsv\")\nvalue <- subset(summary, method == \"ED-PPO\" & env == \"Humanoid\")$mean[[1]]\n```\n\nYAML:\n```yaml\n# @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nreported_metric: mnist_accuracy_mean\n```\n\nJSON sidecar:\n```json\n{\n  \"schema_name\": \"scientific-evidence.artifact-manifest\",\n  \"artifact\": {\"path\": \"figures/main.png\", \"kind\": \"figure\"},\n  \"derived_from\": [\n    \"@evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\",\n    \"@evidence[[scripts/build_figure.py]]\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Verification\n\nSmoke test the script, bundle generation, and verifier:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\n```\n---\nname: memory-log\ndescription: Maintain a continuous, append-only `./memory.md` log of discoveries, evaluations, failures, and next hypotheses during agent runs.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nKeep durable run memory so idea evolution and program evidence are never lost between steps.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- At run start to initialize memory.\n- After every meaningful step (`idea`, `edit`, `evaluate`, `verify`, `paper`, `draft`).\n- Immediately after every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair.\n\n# What to do\n1. Initialize `./memory.md` if missing with these section headers:\n- `# Memory Log`\n- `## Context`\n- `## Idea Discoveries`\n- `## Program Evaluations`\n- `## Failures / Regressions`\n- `## Next Hypotheses`\n2. Also initialize global files if missing:\n- `./global_memory/memory.md`\n- `./global_memory/memory.jsonl`\n3. Never delete or rewrite prior entries; append only.\n4. For each appended entry, include:\n- timestamp\n- step type\n- change/discovery summary\n- concrete evidence\n- next action\n5. After every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair, append a `Program Evaluations` entry that includes:\n- `program_<idx>`\n- `combined_score`\n- `correct`\n- rubric pass/fail summary\n- `next_recommendation`\n6. For regressions or failed rubrics, append one root-cause hypothesis under `Failures / Regressions`.\n7. Before exit, append final status (`rubric_pass`, `cap_reached`, or task-specific stop reason) and next hypothesis under `Next Hypotheses`.\n8. When command execution is unavailable, use read/edit/write tools to append directly\nto the required markdown and JSONL files. Preserve all existing content. When command\nexecution is available, prefer the helper for deterministic dual-write:\n- script: detect the active agent config directory first, then use `<that-dir>/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py`\n- command template:\n```bash\nMEMORY_HELPER=\"$(find ./.claude ./.codex ./.cursor ./.opencode ./.gemini ./.pi -path '*/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py' -print -quit 2>/dev/null)\"\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" \\\n  --global-memory-md \"./global_memory/memory.md\" \\\n  --global-memory-jsonl \"./global_memory/memory.jsonl\" \\\n  --event-json '{\"step\":\"evaluate\",\"scope\":\"attempt\",\"summary\":\"...\",\"evidence\":\"...\",\"next_action\":\"...\",\"attempt_id\":\"{{ATTEMPT_ID}}\",\"idea_id\":\"{{IDEA_ID}}\",\"program_id\":\"program_2\",\"metrics\":{\"combined_score\":2.54,\"correct\":true}}'\n```\n9. JSONL event schema (required keys):\n- `timestamp` (auto-filled if omitted)\n- `step` (`idea|edit|evaluate|verify|paper|draft|final|conclusions_skip`)\n- `scope` (`idea|attempt|paper|campaign`)\n- `summary`\n- `evidence`\n- `next_action`\n- optional: `idea_id`, `attempt_id`, `program_id`, `metrics`, `rubrics`, `tags`\n\n10. Update distilled conclusions (`conclusions.md`).\n**Purpose.** `conclusions.md` is a *forward-looking guide* for the next worker (or your future self in a later cycle). Its job is to help readers (a) focus their effort on directions you've found promising, and (b) avoid spending compute on directions you've already shown to be unpromising. Each sentence should either point at a lead worth pursuing or warn against a dead end, framed so a researcher who has not read your `memory.md` can still act on it. Avoid chronology, restate-of-results, and post-mortem narration — those belong in `memory.md`. Conclusions are the synthesis layer that makes other workers' next cycles more efficient.\n\n**When.** After every memory-append in the cycle (idea / edit / evaluate / verify / paper / final), evaluate whether `conclusions.md` should be updated.\n\n**Update gate (the only valid triggers).**\n- (a) cycle **contradicts** an existing sentence → rewrite that sentence.\n- (b) cycle opens a **new category** not yet covered → add a sentence.\n- (c) cycle **flips a Do/Don't** recommendation → flip it.\n- (d) cycle **meaningfully refines** an existing sentence — narrows the conditions under which a rule holds, broadens its applicability to a config it wasn't known to apply to, sharpens a tentative claim into a confident one, or adds a qualifier the prior phrasing didn't cover → edit the sentence to incorporate the refinement. Simple re-confirmation in the same regime is **not** sufficient; you must identify the refinement dimension (narrow / broaden / sharpen / qualify) in `--gate-reason`.\n- otherwise → **keep**, do not invoke the helper.\n\n**Format constraints.** Single paragraph. ≤300 words. No blank lines (`\\n\\n`). Avoid detailed numerical figures — use qualitative thresholds (e.g. \"low LR\", \"small batch\") instead of precise floats like `LR=0.205`.\n\n**Update mechanism.** When execution is unavailable, apply the same validation rules\nand update the conclusions files directly with read/edit/write tools. When execution\nis available, use this helper invocation:\n```bash\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" --write-conclusions \\\n  --conclusions-md \"{{CONCLUSIONS_MD_FILE}}\" \\\n  --conclusions-history-jsonl \"{{CONCLUSIONS_HISTORY_FILE}}\" \\\n  --cycle-index {{CYCLE_INDEX}} \\\n  --worker-index {{WORKER_INDEX}} \\\n  --gate-trigger <a|b|c|d> \\\n  --gate-reason \"<one sentence; for (d) name the dimension>\" \\\n  --conclusions-text \"<the full new paragraph>\"\n```\n\n**Pre-flight self-check.** Before invoking the helper, count words (`len(paragraph.split())`) and confirm ≤300, and that the paragraph contains no blank line. Most validation rejections can be avoided here.\n\n**Retry budget — exactly 3 attempts per cycle.** On non-zero exit, read the stderr error, shorten the paragraph (drop the least-decisive sentence, merge two related sentences, or remove qualifiers), and retry. After **3 total attempts** with no success, the update is **discarded** for this cycle: do not retry further, do not write the rejected text anywhere, and append a single memory event with `step=\"conclusions_skip\"` whose `summary` names the last validation error. Underlying findings are not lost — they are already durable in `memory.md`.\n\n**Always log the gate decision.** Inside the regular memory append for the cycle, include `gate=<updated|kept|skipped>; trigger=<a|b|c|d|none>; reason=<one sentence>` in the entry's `summary` or `tags`. Use `skipped` only after the 3-attempt budget is exhausted.\n\n# Example evaluation entry\n```markdown\n### 2026-02-13T16:58:00Z | step=evaluate\n- program: program_3\n- combined_score: 2.54\n- correct: true\n- rubrics: uses_restarts=pass, constraint_optimization=pass, score_threshold=fail\n- evidence: late-stage overlap still visible in stdout trace\n- next_recommendation: raise late-stage overlap penalty and diversify restart seeds\n```\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_rendered_si.py:2:\"\"\"Validate rendered Supplementary Information in a compiled paper PDF.\"\"\"\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_rendered_si.py:18:    rf\"(?mi)^[ \\t]*(?:(?:appendix[ \\t]+)?[A-Z0-9]+(?:\\.\\d+)*[.:]?[ \\t]+)?\"\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_rendered_si.py:24:    rf\"{_letter_spaced_word_pattern('bibliography')})[ \\t]*$\"\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_rendered_si.py:30:        description=\"Validate rendered Supplementary Information in a PDF.\"\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_rendered_si.py:46:        return [\"compiled paper is missing the Supplementary Information heading\"]\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_rendered_si.py:58:        errors.append(\"compiled paper is missing the References heading before SI\")\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_rendered_si.py:75:            \"compiled Supplementary Information must start on a separate page after References\"\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_rendered_si.py:93:            \"compiled Supplementary Information must contain at least 100 visible characters\"\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_rendered_si.py:117:        raise SystemExit(\"Rendered Supplementary Information validation failed: \" + \"; \".join(errors))\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_rendered_si.py:118:    print(\"Validated rendered Supplementary Information.\")\niclr2026_conference.sty:64:   \\def\\@makefnmark{\\hbox to 0pt{$^{\\@thefnmark}$\\hss}} % for perfect author\niclr2026_conference.sty:66:%   The footnote-mark was overlapping the footnote-text,\niclr2026_conference.sty:69:                            \\hbox to1.8em{\\hss $\\m@th ^{\\@thefnmark}$}##1}\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:2:\"\"\"Validate that paper citations align with bibliography entries.\"\"\"\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:21:        r\"\\\\bibliography\\{|\\\\printbibliography|\\\\begin\\{thebibliography\\}|\\\\section\\*?\\{[^}]*references[^}]*\\}\",\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:32:    r\"\\\\bibliography\\s*\\{[^}]+\\}|\\\\printbibliography\\b(?:\\[[^\\]]*\\])?\"\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:33:    r\"|\\\\begin\\s*\\{thebibliography\\}\",\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:36:CLEARPAGE_RE = re.compile(r\"\\\\clearpage\\b\", re.IGNORECASE)\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:274:        r\"\\\\(?:bibliography|bibliographystyle|label|ref|eqref|pageref|cite[a-zA-Z*]*)\"\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:289:    appendix_matches = list(re.finditer(r\"\\\\appendix\\b\", structural_tex))\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:290:    appendix_before_heading = _last_match_before(appendix_matches, heading_match)\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:291:    appendix_match = appendix_before_heading or (appendix_matches[0] if appendix_matches else None)\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:292:    bibliography_matches = list(BIBLIOGRAPHY_RE.finditer(structural_tex))\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:293:    bibliography_match = bibliography_matches[-1] if bibliography_matches else None\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:294:    section_before_bibliography = _last_match_before(\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:295:        list(NEXT_SECTION_RE.finditer(structural_tex)), bibliography_match\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:298:    if appendix_match is None:\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:299:        errors.append(r\"paper.tex missing \\appendix for Supplementary Information\")\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:301:        errors.append(\"paper.tex missing Supplementary Information heading\")\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:303:    if appendix_match is not None and appendix_match.start() > heading_match.start():\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:304:        errors.append(r\"\\appendix must appear before the Supplementary Information heading\")\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:306:        bibliography_match is None\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:307:        or appendix_before_heading is None\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:308:        or bibliography_match.start() > appendix_before_heading.start()\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:310:        errors.append(\"Supplementary Information must appear after the bibliography\")\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:312:        section_before_bibliography is None\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:314:            structural_tex, section_before_bibliography.end(), bibliography_match.start()\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:318:        errors.append(r\"paper.tex requires \\clearpage before the bibliography\")\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:320:        bibliography_match is not None\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:321:        and appendix_before_heading is not None\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:322:        and bibliography_match.start() < appendix_before_heading.start()\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:324:            structural_tex, bibliography_match.end(), appendix_before_heading.start()\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:329:            r\"Supplementary Information requires \\clearpage between the bibliography and \\appendix\"\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:343:        errors.append(\"Supplementary Information still contains template placeholder text\")\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:345:        errors.append(\"Supplementary Information must contain at least 100 visible characters\")\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:348:            \"Supplementary Information must contain at least one substantive subsection, table, or figure\"\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:353:        and later_section_titles[0] in {\"references\", \"bibliography\"}\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:356:        errors.append(\"Supplementary Information must be the final top-level section\")\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:360:        errors.append(\"Supplementary Information must not use dynamic top-level sections\")\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:362:        errors.append(\"Supplementary Information must not import unchecked TeX files\")\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py:449:            \"Supplementary Information validation failed: \" + \"; \".join(supplementary_errors)\npaper.tex:2:% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\npaper.tex:5:\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\npaper.tex:6:\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\npaper.tex:8:  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\npaper.tex:9:  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\npaper.tex:10:  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\npaper.tex:11:  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\npaper.tex:12:  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\npaper.tex:13:  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\npaper.tex:14:  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\npaper.tex:15:  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\npaper.tex:17:% --- end watermark ---\npaper.tex:24:\\title{Local Residual Transfer for Budgeted\\\\Embedding Benchmark Prediction}\npaper.tex:31:Predicting an embedding model's benchmark profile from a small evaluation budget requires both selecting informative benchmarks and transferring information from incomplete historical results. We study a low-rank predictor augmented with cross-fitted, locally weighted residual medians, evaluated on a pinned public MTEB score matrix. The predictor chooses queries by integrated variance reduction and calibrates residual transfer using historical models only. Across model and heuristic-family holdouts, three statistical seeds, and budgets of one, two, four, and eight queried task/subset columns, its aggregate mean absolute error is 0.04081, compared with 0.04525 for the strongest aggregate baseline. Error decreases from 0.04523 at one query to 0.03615 at eight. Historical-data disruptions delimit this finding: independent column shifts remove the advantage over column means. These results support the evaluated configuration on previously observed leaderboard cells, not causal attribution to residual transfer, prediction of genuinely missing results, or a universally sufficient evaluation budget.\npaper.tex:37:Embedding evaluations measure different aspects of a representation, so a single score does not necessarily characterize performance on retrieval, semantic similarity, classification, and other uses. MTEB provides a public framework for evaluating this breadth \\citep{MTEB}. Running a broad evaluation suite for every new model, however, is expensive. Historical score tables suggest a complementary approach: evaluate a new model on a small set of task columns and infer its remaining profile from relationships learned across previously evaluated models. This paper examines the statistical prediction problem, not the execution cost of the underlying benchmark workloads.\npaper.tex:61:MTEB motivates studying broad embedding profiles rather than a single similarity task \\citep{MTEB}. Neighboring work reduces evaluation at a different granularity: tinyBenchmarks selects small sets of examples, while metabench compresses reasoning and knowledge assessments into sparse item sets \\citep{TinyBenchmarks,MetaBench}. Here the acquisition unit is an entire task/subset score, not an item response. Observational scaling laws and Sloth model performance through low-dimensional capabilities or skills across model families \\citep{ObservationalScaling,Sloth}. They motivate compact cross-benchmark structure, but our experiment neither fits a compute-scaling law nor tests those approaches as baselines. In particular, evidence from generative-model evaluations cannot establish the present embedding predictor's accuracy.\npaper.tex:64:Factor models and neighborhood methods are established tools for collaborative prediction \\citep{Koren2009,Takacs2008}. Probabilistic and Bayesian factorization formalize latent-factor modeling and uncertainty over parameters \\citep{Mnih2007,Salakhutdinov2008}; the present ALS estimator instead uses point estimates. Work on implicit feedback emphasizes observation semantics and confidence \\citep{Hu2008}, whereas the present mask denotes an available benchmark score, not an implicit preference. Convex matrix completion, alternating minimization, and maximum-margin factorization supply distinct approaches to incomplete matrices \\citep{Candes2009,Jain2013,Rennie2005}. Their recovery or optimization results are not guarantees for this selected leaderboard matrix. Local prediction has classical roots in neighbor classification and nonparametric regression \\citep{Cover1967,Stone1977}; quantile regression connects medians with absolute-error objectives \\citep{Koenker2001}. These connections motivate residual transfer without certifying its finite-sample behavior here.\npaper.tex:79:The design combines a global model with a conditional residual estimator. Global factors share information even when direct benchmark overlap is limited. Local donor profiles can represent deviations from the global fit, but missing anchors weaken neighborhood identification. We therefore use overlap-aware weights, donor-excluded predictions, effective-count shrinkage, and historical validation with a null correction. These are safeguards in the estimator, not evidence that all remaining bias or leakage is eliminated.\npaper.tex:81:\\subsection{Global fit and benchmark acquisition}\npaper.tex:140:The retained final matrix has 126 rows and 135 columns, where a column identifies task, dataset revision, split, subset, and metric. These are not 135 independent benchmark suites. Extraction retains supported English text-only main metrics, checks their agreement with the reported main score, and chooses a common supported revision/metric/split per task/subset under the recorded coverage rule. Per-model revision selection favors coverage. Bounded noncorrelation scores remain in $[0,1]$; correlations are mapped by $(x+1)/2$. Hence errors are in normalized score units, not percentage-point errors uniformly comparable across raw metrics.\npaper.tex:248:\\clearpage\npaper.tex:249:\\bibliography{references}\npaper.tex:250:\\bibliographystyle{iclr2026_conference}\npaper.tex:252:\\clearpage\npaper.tex:253:\\appendix\npaper.tex:254:\\section{Supplementary Information}\npaper.tex:262:The manifest records CC0-1.0 evidence for results and Apache-2.0 for metadata, but also records that the results license was absent from the pinned results snapshot. Thus the available current-license evidence should not be represented as an unambiguous historical license grant at that snapshot. Public access and the presence of a repository license do not resolve all underlying benchmark-data terms. The bundled source documents and their hashes permit checking the matrix without downloading repository history; upstream acquisition should use the exact revision recorded in the manifest.\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_compile_inputs.py:201:def _validate_canonical_bibliography_target(root: Path, target_text: str) -> None:\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_compile_inputs.py:203:        raise RuntimeError(\"paper.aux uses a dynamic bibliography target\")\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_compile_inputs.py:210:        raise RuntimeError(\"compiled paper must use the canonical references.bib bibliography\")\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_compile_inputs.py:212:        raise RuntimeError(\"compiled paper bibliography must not use symlinks\")\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_compile_inputs.py:215:def _validate_bibliography_style_target(root: Path, target_text: str) -> None:\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_compile_inputs.py:217:        raise RuntimeError(\"paper.aux uses a dynamic bibliography style target\")\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_compile_inputs.py:227:        raise RuntimeError(\"compiled bibliography style escapes the paper workspace\") from exc\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_compile_inputs.py:229:        raise RuntimeError(\"compiled paper must not use agent skill bibliography styles\")\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_compile_inputs.py:231:        raise RuntimeError(f\"compiled local bibliography style is missing: {candidate}\")\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_compile_inputs.py:233:        raise RuntimeError(\"compiled bibliography style must not use symlinks\")\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_compile_inputs.py:254:            \"paper.aux must declare exactly one bibliography database and style\"\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_compile_inputs.py:257:        _validate_canonical_bibliography_target(root, target.strip())\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_compile_inputs.py:258:    _validate_bibliography_style_target(root, style_matches[0].group(\"target\").strip())\nimport importlib.util\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport re\nimport shutil\nimport subprocess\n\nimport jsonschema\nimport pymupdf\n\n\ndef main():\n    \"\"\"@evidence[[paper.tex]]\n    @evidence[[figure_manifest.json]]\n    @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation]]\n    \"\"\"\n    report = dict(checks={}, canonical_limitations=[])\n    manifest = json.loads(Path('figure_manifest.json').read_text())\n    schema = json.loads(Path('.codex/skills/validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json').read_text())\n    jsonschema.validate(manifest, schema)\n    report['checks']['figure_schema'] = True\n    strategy = json.loads(Path('paper_strategy.json').read_text())\n    specifications = {entry['figure_id']: entry for entry in strategy['figure_specs']}\n    tex = Path('paper.tex').read_text()\n    referenced = re.findall(r'\\\\includegraphics(?:\\[[^\\]]*\\])?\\{([^}]+)\\}', tex)\n    for figure in manifest['figures']:\n        spec = specifications[figure['figure_id']]\n        assert figure['figure_type'] == spec['figure_type']\n        assert figure['n_panels'] <= spec['max_panels']\n        assert figure['panels_per_row'] <= spec['max_panels_per_row']\n        assert max(figure['categories_per_panel']) <= spec['max_categories_per_panel']\n        assert max(figure['series_per_panel']) <= spec['max_series_per_panel']\n        for key in ['path_pdf', 'path_png', 'prompt_log_path']:\n            if figure.get(key):\n                assert Path(figure[key]).is_file()\n        used = figure.get('path_pdf') or figure['path_png']\n        assert used in referenced\n        if figure['generator'] == 'matplotlib':\n            assert figure['estimated_min_rendered_font_pt'] >= 8.5\n    assert len(referenced) == len(manifest['figures'])\n    report['checks']['figure_assets_and_strategy_budgets'] = True\n    report['concept_render_status'] = 'rendered' if Path('.concept_figure_complete').exists() else 'placeholder; orchestrator rendering pending by contract'\n    report['canonical_limitations'].append('Canonical validate_figures.py cannot import the unavailable src.orchestrator package; local JSON Schema, asset, density and placement checks are reported separately.')\n    pdf = pymupdf.open('paper.pdf')\n    page_texts = [page.get_text() for page in pdf]\n    references = next(index for index, text in enumerate(page_texts) if re.search(r'(?m)^REFERENCES\\s*$', text))\n    assert 8 <= references <= 9\n    blocks = [block for block in pdf[references - 1].get_text('blocks') if block[0] >= 100 and block[1] >= 75 and block[3] < 745]\n    bottom = max(block[3] for block in blocks)\n    occupancy = (bottom - 80) / 648\n    assert references != 8 or occupancy >= .75\n    report['checks']['body_pages'] = references\n    report['checks']['last_body_page_geometric_vertical_fraction'] = occupancy\n    report['checks']['total_pages'] = len(pdf)\n    visible = '\\n'.join(page_texts)\n    forbidden = r'\\b(?:idea|ideas|program|programs|attempt|attempts|eligible|worker_01|worker_02|combined_score|rubric)\\b'\n    assert not re.search(forbidden, visible, re.I)\n    assert 'Anonymous authors' in visible\n    assert not re.search(r'(?mi)^\\s*(?:acknowledg\\w*|funding)\\s*$', visible)\n    report['checks']['visible_language_and_anonymity'] = True\n    si_path = Path('.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_rendered_si.py')\n    specification = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location('rendered_si', si_path)\n    module = importlib.util.module_from_spec(specification)\n    specification.loader.exec_module(module)\n    if shutil.which('pdftotext'):\n        rendered = subprocess.run(['pdftotext', '-layout', 'paper.pdf', '-'], check=True, capture_output=True, text=True)\n        rendered_pages = rendered.stdout.split('\\f')\n        report['rendered_supplement_extraction_backend'] = 'pdftotext'\n    else:\n        rendered_pages = []\n        for page in pdf:\n            rows = {}\n            for block in page.get_text('dict')['blocks']:\n                for line in block.get('lines', []):\n                    if line['dir'] != (1.0, 0.0) or line['bbox'][0] < 100:\n                        continue\n                    text = ''.join(span['text'] for span in line['spans'])\n                    rows.setdefault(round(line['bbox'][1], 1), []).append((line['bbox'][0], text))\n            rendered_pages.append('\\n'.join(' '.join(text for left, text in sorted(parts)) for top, parts in sorted(rows.items())))\n        report['rendered_supplement_extraction_backend'] = 'PyMuPDF horizontal text rows, excluding margin line numbers and diagonal watermark'\n        report['canonical_limitations'].append('Canonical compile workflow requires unavailable pdftotext; the rendered supplement is independently checked using PyMuPDF extraction.')\n    assert not module.validate_rendered_supplementary_information(rendered_pages)\n    report['checks']['rendered_supplement'] = True\n    for stem in ['budget_details', 'seed_details']:\n        assert Path('tables/' + stem + '.tex').read_text().strip() in tex\n    report['checks']['embedded_supplement_tables_equal_generated_tables'] = True\n    for target, pointer in re.findall(r'@evidence\\[\\[([^#\\]]+)#(/[^\\]]*)\\]\\]', tex):\n        if target.endswith('.json'):\n            value = json.loads(Path(target).read_text())\n            for part in pointer[1:].split('/'):\n                part = part.replace('~1', '/').replace('~0', '~')\n                value = value[int(part)] if isinstance(value, list) else value[part]\n    report['checks']['manuscript_json_pointers_resolve'] = True\n    log = Path('paper.log').read_text()\n    assert 'undefined' not in log.lower()\n    assert 'Overfull' not in log\n    report['checks']['no_unresolved_tex_references_or_overfull_boxes'] = True\n    bibkeys = re.findall(r'@\\w+\\{([^,]+),', Path('references.bib').read_text())\n    assert 25 <= len(bibkeys) <= 30\n    report['checks']['reference_count'] = len(bibkeys)\n    claims = json.loads(Path('claims_grounding.json').read_text())\n    report['checks']['mapped_claims'] = len(claims['claims'])\n    gate = subprocess.run(['python3', '.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_compile_inputs.py', 'recorder', '--paper-dir', '.', '--fls', 'paper.fls'], capture_output=True, text=True)\n    report['canonical_compile_input_gate'] = dict(passed=gate.returncode == 0, message=(gate.stdout + gate.stderr).strip())\n    if gate.returncode:\n        report['canonical_limitations'].append('Canonical recorder gate rejects Debian system texmf.cnf symlink. Direct compilation and independent resolved-path dependency checks succeeded; canonical gate is not reported as passed.')\n        for line in Path('paper.fls').read_text().splitlines():\n            if line.startswith('INPUT '):\n                path = Path(line[6:]).resolve()\n                assert path.is_relative_to(Path.cwd()) or any(path.is_relative_to(Path(root)) for root in ['/usr/share/texlive', '/etc/texmf', '/var/lib/texmf']), str(path)\n    report['checks']['resolved_compilation_inputs_within_workspace_or_system_tex_roots'] = True\n    Path('local_validation_report.json').write_text(json.dumps(report, indent=2))\n    for index in [0, 1, references - 1, references + 2]:\n        pdf[index].get_pixmap(matrix=pymupdf.Matrix(1.3, 1.3)).save('/tmp/manuscript_page_' + str(index + 1) + '.png')\n    print(json.dumps(report, indent=2))\n\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\n#!/bin/sh\nset -eu\npdflatex -recorder -no-shell-escape -interaction=nonstopmode -halt-on-error paper.tex\nbibtex paper\npdflatex -recorder -no-shell-escape -interaction=nonstopmode -halt-on-error paper.tex\npdflatex -recorder -no-shell-escape -interaction=nonstopmode -halt-on-error paper.tex\nnode-compile-cache\nopencode\n{\n  \"audit_json_path\": \"work/results/r03/codex/20261117_111425/paper/paper_evidence_audit.json\",\n  \"audit_markdown_path\": \"work/results/r03/codex/20261117_111425/paper/paper_evidence_audit.md\",\n  \"finding_count\": 0,\n  \"findings\": [],\n  \"generated_at\": \"2026-11-17T14:53:48.061510+00:00\",\n  \"ok\": true,\n  \"raw_output_path\": \"work/results/r03/codex/20261117_111425/paper/paper_evidence_audit_raw.txt\",\n  \"summary\": \"Main and supplementary claims agree with the retained results, implementation evidence, and qualification record, while preserving attribution and generalization limitations.\",\n  \"verdict\": \"pass\"\n}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"rg -n 'Supplementary|References|bibliography|mark|appendix|clearpage' paper.tex iclr2026_conference.sty; rg -n 'def validate_rendered|separate page|reference' .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py; ls .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts; cat VENUE.md; sed -n '1,100p' check_paper_locally.py; ls /opt; which python3; env | rg 'VENV|TMPDIR|PATH'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"rg -n 'Supplementary|References|bibliography|mark|appendix|clearpage' paper.tex iclr2026_conference.sty; rg -n 'def validate_rendered|separate page|reference' .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py; ls .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts; cat VENUE.md; sed -n '1,100p' check_paper_locally.py; ls /opt; which python3; env | rg 'VENV|TMPDIR|PATH'\"","aggregated_output":"iclr2026_conference.sty:64:   \\def\\@makefnmark{\\hbox to 0pt{$^{\\@thefnmark}$\\hss}} % for perfect author\niclr2026_conference.sty:66:%   The footnote-mark was overlapping the footnote-text,\niclr2026_conference.sty:69:                            \\hbox to1.8em{\\hss $\\m@th ^{\\@thefnmark}$}##1}\npaper.tex:2:% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\npaper.tex:5:\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\npaper.tex:6:\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\npaper.tex:8:  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\npaper.tex:9:  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\npaper.tex:10:  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\npaper.tex:11:  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\npaper.tex:12:  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\npaper.tex:13:  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\npaper.tex:14:  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\npaper.tex:15:  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\npaper.tex:17:% --- end watermark ---\npaper.tex:24:\\title{Local Residual Transfer for Budgeted\\\\Embedding Benchmark Prediction}\npaper.tex:31:Predicting an embedding model's benchmark profile from a small evaluation budget requires both selecting informative benchmarks and transferring information from incomplete historical results. We study a low-rank predictor augmented with cross-fitted, locally weighted residual medians, evaluated on a pinned public MTEB score matrix. The predictor chooses queries by integrated variance reduction and calibrates residual transfer using historical models only. Across model and heuristic-family holdouts, three statistical seeds, and budgets of one, two, four, and eight queried task/subset columns, its aggregate mean absolute error is 0.04081, compared with 0.04525 for the strongest aggregate baseline. Error decreases from 0.04523 at one query to 0.03615 at eight. Historical-data disruptions delimit this finding: independent column shifts remove the advantage over column means. These results support the evaluated configuration on previously observed leaderboard cells, not causal attribution to residual transfer, prediction of genuinely missing results, or a universally sufficient evaluation budget.\npaper.tex:37:Embedding evaluations measure different aspects of a representation, so a single score does not necessarily characterize performance on retrieval, semantic similarity, classification, and other uses. MTEB provides a public framework for evaluating this breadth \\citep{MTEB}. Running a broad evaluation suite for every new model, however, is expensive. Historical score tables suggest a complementary approach: evaluate a new model on a small set of task columns and infer its remaining profile from relationships learned across previously evaluated models. This paper examines the statistical prediction problem, not the execution cost of the underlying benchmark workloads.\npaper.tex:61:MTEB motivates studying broad embedding profiles rather than a single similarity task \\citep{MTEB}. Neighboring work reduces evaluation at a different granularity: tinyBenchmarks selects small sets of examples, while metabench compresses reasoning and knowledge assessments into sparse item sets \\citep{TinyBenchmarks,MetaBench}. Here the acquisition unit is an entire task/subset score, not an item response. Observational scaling laws and Sloth model performance through low-dimensional capabilities or skills across model families \\citep{ObservationalScaling,Sloth}. They motivate compact cross-benchmark structure, but our experiment neither fits a compute-scaling law nor tests those approaches as baselines. In particular, evidence from generative-model evaluations cannot establish the present embedding predictor's accuracy.\npaper.tex:64:Factor models and neighborhood methods are established tools for collaborative prediction \\citep{Koren2009,Takacs2008}. Probabilistic and Bayesian factorization formalize latent-factor modeling and uncertainty over parameters \\citep{Mnih2007,Salakhutdinov2008}; the present ALS estimator instead uses point estimates. Work on implicit feedback emphasizes observation semantics and confidence \\citep{Hu2008}, whereas the present mask denotes an available benchmark score, not an implicit preference. Convex matrix completion, alternating minimization, and maximum-margin factorization supply distinct approaches to incomplete matrices \\citep{Candes2009,Jain2013,Rennie2005}. Their recovery or optimization results are not guarantees for this selected leaderboard matrix. Local prediction has classical roots in neighbor classification and nonparametric regression \\citep{Cover1967,Stone1977}; quantile regression connects medians with absolute-error objectives \\citep{Koenker2001}. These connections motivate residual transfer without certifying its finite-sample behavior here.\npaper.tex:79:The design combines a global model with a conditional residual estimator. Global factors share information even when direct benchmark overlap is limited. Local donor profiles can represent deviations from the global fit, but missing anchors weaken neighborhood identification. We therefore use overlap-aware weights, donor-excluded predictions, effective-count shrinkage, and historical validation with a null correction. These are safeguards in the estimator, not evidence that all remaining bias or leakage is eliminated.\npaper.tex:81:\\subsection{Global fit and benchmark acquisition}\npaper.tex:140:The retained final matrix has 126 rows and 135 columns, where a column identifies task, dataset revision, split, subset, and metric. These are not 135 independent benchmark suites. Extraction retains supported English text-only main metrics, checks their agreement with the reported main score, and chooses a common supported revision/metric/split per task/subset under the recorded coverage rule. Per-model revision selection favors coverage. Bounded noncorrelation scores remain in $[0,1]$; correlations are mapped by $(x+1)/2$. Hence errors are in normalized score units, not percentage-point errors uniformly comparable across raw metrics.\npaper.tex:248:\\clearpage\npaper.tex:249:\\bibliography{references}\npaper.tex:250:\\bibliographystyle{iclr2026_conference}\npaper.tex:252:\\clearpage\npaper.tex:253:\\appendix\npaper.tex:254:\\section{Supplementary Information}\npaper.tex:262:The manifest records CC0-1.0 evidence for results and Apache-2.0 for metadata, but also records that the results license was absent from the pinned results snapshot. Thus the available current-license evidence should not be represented as an unambiguous historical license grant at that snapshot. Public access and the presence of a repository license do not resolve all underlying benchmark-data terms. The bundled source documents and their hashes permit checking the matrix without downloading repository history; upstream acquisition should use the exact revision recorded in the manifest.\n20:    \"references\": re.compile(\n21:        r\"\\\\bibliography\\{|\\\\printbibliography|\\\\begin\\{thebibliography\\}|\\\\section\\*?\\{[^}]*references[^}]*\\}\",\n353:        and later_section_titles[0] in {\"references\", \"bibliography\"}\n468:    print(f\"Validated sections and {len(cited_keys)} citation key references.\")\n__pycache__\ncompile_latex.sh\nvalidate_compile_inputs.py\nvalidate_figures.py\nvalidate_rendered_si.py\nvalidate_tex_refs.py\n# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.\n- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.\n- Do not omit baselines or ablations that the grounded artifacts already support.\n- Do not describe results as state of the art unless the comparison in the artifacts establishes it.\n- Do not pad the paper to fill the page budget.\n\n## Decision Rule\n- When venue framing and grounded evidence conflict, follow the grounded evidence.\n- Use this brief to choose emphasis, terminology, and narrative framing, not to invent stronger claims\n  than the run supports.\nimport importlib.util\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport re\nimport shutil\nimport subprocess\n\nimport jsonschema\nimport pymupdf\n\n\ndef main():\n    \"\"\"@evidence[[paper.tex]]\n    @evidence[[figure_manifest.json]]\n    @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation]]\n    \"\"\"\n    report = dict(checks={}, canonical_limitations=[])\n    manifest = json.loads(Path('figure_manifest.json').read_text())\n    schema = json.loads(Path('.codex/skills/validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json').read_text())\n    jsonschema.validate(manifest, schema)\n    report['checks']['figure_schema'] = True\n    strategy = json.loads(Path('paper_strategy.json').read_text())\n    specifications = {entry['figure_id']: entry for entry in strategy['figure_specs']}\n    tex = Path('paper.tex').read_text()\n    referenced = re.findall(r'\\\\includegraphics(?:\\[[^\\]]*\\])?\\{([^}]+)\\}', tex)\n    for figure in manifest['figures']:\n        spec = specifications[figure['figure_id']]\n        assert figure['figure_type'] == spec['figure_type']\n        assert figure['n_panels'] <= spec['max_panels']\n        assert figure['panels_per_row'] <= spec['max_panels_per_row']\n        assert max(figure['categories_per_panel']) <= spec['max_categories_per_panel']\n        assert max(figure['series_per_panel']) <= spec['max_series_per_panel']\n        for key in ['path_pdf', 'path_png', 'prompt_log_path']:\n            if figure.get(key):\n                assert Path(figure[key]).is_file()\n        used = figure.get('path_pdf') or figure['path_png']\n        assert used in referenced\n        if figure['generator'] == 'matplotlib':\n            assert figure['estimated_min_rendered_font_pt'] >= 8.5\n    assert len(referenced) == len(manifest['figures'])\n    report['checks']['figure_assets_and_strategy_budgets'] = True\n    report['concept_render_status'] = 'rendered' if Path('.concept_figure_complete').exists() else 'placeholder; orchestrator rendering pending by contract'\n    report['canonical_limitations'].append('Canonical validate_figures.py cannot import the unavailable src.orchestrator package; local JSON Schema, asset, density and placement checks are reported separately.')\n    pdf = pymupdf.open('paper.pdf')\n    page_texts = [page.get_text() for page in pdf]\n    references = next(index for index, text in enumerate(page_texts) if re.search(r'(?m)^REFERENCES\\s*$', text))\n    assert 8 <= references <= 9\n    blocks = [block for block in pdf[references - 1].get_text('blocks') if block[0] >= 100 and block[1] >= 75 and block[3] < 745]\n    bottom = max(block[3] for block in blocks)\n    occupancy = (bottom - 80) / 648\n    assert references != 8 or occupancy >= .75\n    report['checks']['body_pages'] = references\n    report['checks']['last_body_page_geometric_vertical_fraction'] = occupancy\n    report['checks']['total_pages'] = len(pdf)\n    visible = '\\n'.join(page_texts)\n    forbidden = r'\\b(?:idea|ideas|program|programs|attempt|attempts|eligible|worker_01|worker_02|combined_score|rubric)\\b'\n    assert not re.search(forbidden, visible, re.I)\n    assert 'Anonymous authors' in visible\n    assert not re.search(r'(?mi)^\\s*(?:acknowledg\\w*|funding)\\s*$', visible)\n    report['checks']['visible_language_and_anonymity'] = True\n    si_path = Path('.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_rendered_si.py')\n    specification = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location('rendered_si', si_path)\n    module = importlib.util.module_from_spec(specification)\n    specification.loader.exec_module(module)\n    if shutil.which('pdftotext'):\n        rendered = subprocess.run(['pdftotext', '-layout', 'paper.pdf', '-'], check=True, capture_output=True, text=True)\n        rendered_pages = rendered.stdout.split('\\f')\n        report['rendered_supplement_extraction_backend'] = 'pdftotext'\n    else:\n        rendered_pages = []\n        for page in pdf:\n            rows = {}\n            for block in page.get_text('dict')['blocks']:\n                for line in block.get('lines', []):\n                    if line['dir'] != (1.0, 0.0) or line['bbox'][0] < 100:\n                        continue\n                    text = ''.join(span['text'] for span in line['spans'])\n                    rows.setdefault(round(line['bbox'][1], 1), []).append((line['bbox'][0], text))\n            rendered_pages.append('\\n'.join(' '.join(text for left, text in sorted(parts)) for top, parts in sorted(rows.items())))\n        report['rendered_supplement_extraction_backend'] = 'PyMuPDF horizontal text rows, excluding margin line numbers and diagonal watermark'\n        report['canonical_limitations'].append('Canonical compile workflow requires unavailable pdftotext; the rendered supplement is independently checked using PyMuPDF extraction.')\n    assert not module.validate_rendered_supplementary_information(rendered_pages)\n    report['checks']['rendered_supplement'] = True\n    for stem in ['budget_details', 'seed_details']:\n        assert Path('tables/' + stem + '.tex').read_text().strip() in tex\n    report['checks']['embedded_supplement_tables_equal_generated_tables'] = True\n    for target, pointer in re.findall(r'@evidence\\[\\[([^#\\]]+)#(/[^\\]]*)\\]\\]', tex):\n        if target.endswith('.json'):\n            value = json.loads(Path(target).read_text())\n            for part in pointer[1:].split('/'):\n                part = part.replace('~1', '/').replace('~0', '~')\n                value = value[int(part)] if isinstance(value, list) else value[part]\n    report['checks']['manuscript_json_pointers_resolve'] = True\n    log = Path('paper.log').read_text()\n    assert 'undefined' not in log.lower()\n    assert 'Overfull' not in log\n    report['checks']['no_unresolved_tex_references_or_overfull_boxes'] = True\n    bibkeys = re.findall(r'@\\w+\\{([^,]+),', Path('references.bib').read_text())\n    assert 25 <= len(bibkeys) <= 30\n    report['checks']['reference_count'] = len(bibkeys)\nautoillustrate-venv\ndrawio\nheadless\n/usr/local/bin/python3\nVENV_PY=/opt/autoillustrate-venv/bin/python\nTMPDIR=/agent-tmp\nPATH=/usr/local/lib/node_modules/@openai/codex/node_modules/@openai/codex-linux-x64/vendor/x86_64-unknown-linux-musl/codex-path:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/local/games:/usr/games\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
