{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"find /agent-tmp -maxdepth 3 -type f -name python; sed -n '1,50p' paper.tex; sed -n '238,258p' paper.tex; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py; cat paper_strategy.json | head -100; python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\npaper=json.loads(Path('paper_data.json').read_text())\nprint('tasks:',paper.get('task_names'))\nprint('downstream:',paper.get('downstream_study'))\nprint('final keys:',paper.get('final_evaluation',{}).keys())\nprint('qualification:',paper.get('final_evaluation',{}).get('qualification'))\nprint('registry qualification:',[(key,value) for key,value in paper['grounding_sources'].items() if key.startswith('final.qualification')])\nfor name in ['pymupdf','matplotlib','jsonschema']:\n try: __import__(name); print(name,'OK')\n except ImportError: print(name,'absent')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"find /agent-tmp -maxdepth 3 -type f -name python; sed -n '1,50p' paper.tex; sed -n '238,258p' paper.tex; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py; cat paper_strategy.json | head -100; python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\npaper=json.loads(Path('paper_data.json').read_text())\nprint('tasks:',paper.get('task_names'))\nprint('downstream:',paper.get('downstream_study'))\nprint('final keys:',paper.get('final_evaluation',{}).keys())\nprint('qualification:',paper.get('final_evaluation',{}).get('qualification'))\nprint('registry qualification:',[(key,value) for key,value in paper['grounding_sources'].items() if key.startswith('final.qualification')])\nfor name in ['pymupdf','matplotlib','jsonschema']:\n try: __import__(name); print(name,'OK')\n except ImportError: print(name,'absent')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"\\documentclass{article}\n% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n}\n% --- end watermark ---\n\n\\usepackage{iclr2026_conference,times}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,booktabs,graphicx,subcaption}\n\\usepackage{hyperref,url}\n\\newcommand{\\clip}{\\operatorname{clip}_{[0,1]}}\n\\newcommand{\\MAE}{\\operatorname{MAE}}\n\\title{Local Residual Transfer for Budgeted\\\\Embedding Benchmark Prediction}\n\\author{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nPredicting an embedding model's benchmark profile from a small evaluation budget requires both selecting informative benchmarks and transferring information from incomplete historical results. We study a low-rank predictor augmented with cross-fitted, locally weighted residual medians, evaluated on a pinned public MTEB score matrix. The predictor chooses queries by integrated variance reduction and calibrates residual transfer using historical models only. Across model and heuristic-family holdouts, three statistical seeds, and budgets of one, two, four, and eight queried task/subset columns, its aggregate mean absolute error is 0.04081, compared with 0.04525 for the strongest aggregate baseline. Error decreases from 0.04523 at one query to 0.03615 at eight. Historical-data disruptions delimit this finding: independent column shifts remove the advantage over column means. These results support the evaluated configuration on previously observed leaderboard cells, not causal attribution to residual transfer, prediction of genuinely missing results, or a universally sufficient evaluation budget.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nEmbedding evaluations measure different aspects of a representation, so a single score does not necessarily characterize performance on retrieval, semantic similarity, classification, and other uses. MTEB provides a public framework for evaluating this breadth \\citep{MTEB}. Running a broad evaluation suite for every new model, however, is expensive. Historical score tables suggest a complementary approach: evaluate a new model on a small set of task columns and infer its remaining profile from relationships learned across previously evaluated models. This paper examines the statistical prediction problem, not the execution cost of the underlying benchmark workloads.\n\nTwo questions must be separated. Which columns should an evaluator measure under a fixed budget? And how should the resulting observations be converted into predictions? Low-rank structure offers a compact global description, but models with similar global representations may still share systematic residual errors. A local adjustment could exploit these similarities, while incomplete overlap and small donor neighborhoods could also make it unreliable. A useful study must therefore compare the complete predictor against meaningful alternatives, keep evaluation targets independent of the query policy, and test what happens when historical relationships are disrupted.\n\nWe investigate a concrete package: ridge-regularized alternating least squares (ALS), a greedy integrated-variance query policy, and a historical-only residual calibration procedure. For a new model, the residual adjustment uses historical donors that share queried columns, weights them by profile similarity and overlap, and transfers a shrunk weighted median of their held-out residuals. The design is deliberately conservative: historical validation can choose an exactly zero correction. We hypothesize that this package will lower absolute prediction error relative to simple means, low-rank references, and linear transfer in the observed-score regime. We do not hypothesize that the residual term alone causes any measured gain.\n\nThe evidence comes from one frozen embedding dataset, not a collection of unrelated leaderboards. Its final matrix contains 126 model rows and 135 task/subset columns. Under both model and heuristic-family holdouts, predictions are scored on a common sealed target set that remains unchanged as more queries are revealed. The aggregate MAE advantage over the strongest aggregate baseline is 0.00444 in normalized score units, or 9.82\\% relative to that baseline. The same package loses its advantage over column means when historical column alignments are independently shifted. This boundary finding is part of the conclusion, rather than an exception to be excluded.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/resources/final/manifest.json]]\n% @evidence[[derived_results.json#/relative_reduction]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/delta_best_baseline]]\n\nOur contributions are a fully specified budgeted predictor, a fixed-target evaluation with dependence-aware paired uncertainty, and a measured failure boundary. Factorization, local weighting, and design have established antecedents; we do not claim novelty for these primitives or optimality of the complete configuration.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\paragraph{Selection and practical use.}\nThe evaluated configuration was frozen after development comparisons, so those comparisons are not independent confirmation or controlled component tests. A separate support-gating variant did not yield a retained numeric performance result; its failure cause is not diagnosed and cannot be counted as a measured loss. The final qualification passed, but this does not erase selection uncertainty or the limitations above. For deployment, the appropriate fallback depends on uncertainty about historical comparability, not only the intact-matrix aggregate. The current evidence motivates testing such decisions; it does not validate a switching rule or establish end-to-end savings.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/all_programs]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.qualification.outcome]]\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nBudgeted embedding evaluation can be studied as prediction from an incomplete historical score matrix with controlled access to a held-out model. In the retained MTEB evaluation, a low-rank predictor with historically calibrated local residual transfer has lower aggregate sealed-target MAE than the five evaluated references, and its aggregate error decreases over the tested budgets. Fixed targets make these differences interpretable without letting acquisition remove difficult evaluation cells, while component-paired resampling preserves some important dependencies.\n\nDisrupting historical column alignment removes the advantage over column means, and the comparisons do not isolate a residual-specific causal effect. Observed-cell prediction with heuristic family separation does not establish recovery of genuinely missing scores, transfer to generative models, or a generally sufficient budget. The finding is a configuration-level advantage with an explicit failure boundary: historical score structure can support compact evaluation, but its suitability must be examined rather than assumed.\n\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{iclr2026_conference}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\\subsection{Observed-score extraction and split provenance}\nThe final data manifest records selection of a modal supported dataset revision, split, and metric per task/subset with at least 32 rows globally, followed by a maximal-coverage model revision and a minimum of 12 cells per retained row. Normalized task/version/score content is canonicalized using Unicode normalization, whitespace normalization, sorted keys, and integral-number canonicalization while excluding source identifiers and timing. The recorded allocation uses family lexical-lineage heuristics and document/row duplicate closure, then assigns components to disjoint development and final partitions. The final evaluator re-extracts scores from retained source documents and checks content integrity, alias overlap, and split constraints. These checks are inspectable in the accompanying source; the fingerprints alone do not substantiate extraction semantics.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/resources/final/manifest.json]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L666-L770]]\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Append validated local/global memory entries for idea workflows.\n\nTwo modes:\n\n* default (event append) — appends one memory event to ``memory.md`` (local +\n  global) plus one JSONL record to ``memory.jsonl``. Existing behavior.\n* ``--write-conclusions`` — atomically replaces ``conclusions.md`` with a\n  validated single-paragraph distilled summary and appends a record to\n  ``conclusions_history.jsonl``. Validation rejects empty input,\n  multi-paragraph input, or input over 300 words.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport fcntl\nimport json\nimport os\nimport stat\nimport sys\nfrom datetime import datetime, timezone\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom typing import Any\n\nCONCLUSIONS_WORD_LIMIT = 300\nGATE_TRIGGER_VALUES = (\"a\", \"b\", \"c\", \"d\")\n\nLOCAL_MEMORY_HEADERS = \"\"\"# Memory Log\n\n## Context\n## Idea Discoveries\n## Program Evaluations\n## Failures / Regressions\n## Next Hypotheses\n\"\"\"\n\nGLOBAL_MEMORY_HEADER = \"# Global Memory Log\\n\\n\"\n\n\ndef utc_now() -> str:\n    \"\"\"Return the current UTC timestamp string.\"\"\"\n    return datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat()\n\n\ndef _load_event(*, event_json: str | None, event_file: Path | None) -> dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Load event.\"\"\"\n    if event_json:\n        parsed = json.loads(event_json)\n    elif event_file is not None:\n        parsed = json.loads(event_file.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\"))\n    else:\n        raw = input().strip()\n        if not raw:\n            raise ValueError(\"missing event payload\")\n        parsed = json.loads(raw)\n    if not isinstance(parsed, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"event payload must be a JSON object\")\n    return parsed\n\n\ndef _ensure_required_fields(event: dict[str, Any]) -> dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Ensure required fields.\"\"\"\n    normalized = dict(event)\n    if not isinstance(normalized.get(\"timestamp\"), str) or not normalized[\"timestamp\"].strip():\n        normalized[\"timestamp\"] = utc_now()\n\n    required_string_fields = (\"step\", \"scope\", \"summary\", \"evidence\", \"next_action\")\n    for key in required_string_fields:\n        value = normalized.get(key)\n        if not isinstance(value, str) or not value.strip():\n            raise ValueError(f\"missing required string field: {key}\")\n        normalized[key] = value.strip()\n\n    for key in (\"idea_id\", \"attempt_id\", \"program_id\"):\n        value = normalized.get(key)\n        if value is None:\n            continue\n        if not isinstance(value, str):\n            normalized[key] = str(value)\n        normalized[key] = normalized[key].strip() or None\n\n    metrics = normalized.get(\"metrics\")\n    if metrics is not None and not isinstance(metrics, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"metrics must be an object when present\")\n    rubrics = normalized.get(\"rubrics\")\n    if rubrics is not None and not isinstance(rubrics, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"rubrics must be an array when present\")\n    tags = normalized.get(\"tags\")\n    if tags is not None:\n        if not isinstance(tags, list):\n            raise ValueError(\"tags must be an array when present\")\n        normalized[\"tags\"] = [str(item).strip() for item in tags if str(item).strip()]\n    return normalized\n\n\ndef _markdown_entry(event: dict[str, Any]) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Handle markdown entry logic for this module.\"\"\"\n    lines = [\n        f\"### {event['timestamp']} | step={event['step']} | scope={event['scope']}\",\n        f\"- summary: {event['summary']}\",\n        f\"- evidence: {event['evidence']}\",\n        f\"- next_action: {event['next_action']}\",\n    ]\n\n    for key in (\"idea_id\", \"attempt_id\", \"program_id\"):\n        value = event.get(key)\n        if isinstance(value, str) and value.strip():\n            lines.append(f\"- {key}: {value.strip()}\")\n\n    metrics = event.get(\"metrics\")\n    if isinstance(metrics, dict) and metrics:\n        lines.append(f\"- metrics: {json.dumps(metrics, sort_keys=True)}\")\n    rubrics = event.get(\"rubrics\")\n    if isinstance(rubrics, list) and rubrics:\n        lines.append(f\"- rubrics: {json.dumps(rubrics, sort_keys=True)}\")\n    tags = event.get(\"tags\")\n    if isinstance(tags, list) and tags:\n        lines.append(f\"- tags: {', '.join(str(item) for item in tags)}\")\n\n    return \"\\n\".join(lines) + \"\\n\\n\"\n\n\ndef _append_text(path: Path, text: str, *, header: str) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Safely initialize and append text to a regular file.\"\"\"\n    fd = _open_regular_for_append(path)\n    try:\n        fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX)\n        size = os.fstat(fd).st_size\n        if not size:\n            _write_all(fd, header.encode(\"utf-8\"))\n        elif os.pread(fd, 1, size - 1) != b\"\\n\":\n            _write_all(fd, b\"\\n\")\n        _write_all(fd, text.encode(\"utf-8\"))\n    finally:\n        try:\n            fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_UN)\n        finally:\n            os.close(fd)\n\n\ndef _append_jsonl(path: Path, payload: dict[str, Any]) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Append one JSON object as a JSONL record.\"\"\"\n    line = (json.dumps(payload, sort_keys=True) + \"\\n\").encode(\"utf-8\")\n    fd = _open_regular_for_append(path)\n    try:\n        fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX)\n        size = os.fstat(fd).st_size\n        if size and os.pread(fd, 1, size - 1) != b\"\\n\":\n            _write_all(fd, b\"\\n\")\n        _write_all(fd, line)\n    finally:\n        try:\n            fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_UN)\n        finally:\n            os.close(fd)\n\n\ndef _open_regular_for_append(path: Path) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Open an append target without following its parent or leaf.\"\"\"\n    directory_fd = _walk_directory_no_follow(path.parent)\n    try:\n        fd = os.open(\n            path.name,\n            os.O_APPEND\n            | os.O_CREAT\n            | os.O_RDWR\n            | getattr(os, \"O_NOFOLLOW\", 0)\n            | getattr(os, \"O_NONBLOCK\", 0),\n            0o644,\n            dir_fd=directory_fd,\n        )\n    finally:\n        os.close(directory_fd)\n    if stat.S_ISREG(os.fstat(fd).st_mode):\n        return fd\n    os.close(fd)\n    raise OSError(f\"unsafe non-regular append target: {path}\")\n\n\ndef _walk_directory_no_follow(directory: Path) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Create and walk a directory path without following any component.\"\"\"\n    flags = os.O_RDONLY | getattr(os, \"O_DIRECTORY\", 0) | getattr(os, \"O_NOFOLLOW\", 0)\n    if directory.is_absolute():\n        descriptor = os.open(directory.anchor, flags)\n        components = directory.parts[1:]\n    else:\n        descriptor = os.open(\".\", flags)\n        components = directory.parts\n    try:\n        for component in components:\n            if component in (\"\", \".\"):\n                continue\n            try:\n                os.mkdir(component, mode=0o777, dir_fd=descriptor)\n            except FileExistsError:\n                pass\n            next_descriptor = os.open(component, flags, dir_fd=descriptor)\n            os.close(descriptor)\n            descriptor = next_descriptor\n        return descriptor\n    except BaseException:\n        os.close(descriptor)\n        raise\n\n\ndef _write_all(fd: int, data: bytes) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Write every byte or propagate the write failure.\"\"\"\n    remaining = memoryview(data)\n    while remaining:\n        written = os.write(fd, remaining)\n        if written <= 0:\n            raise OSError(\"write returned no progress\")\n        remaining = remaining[written:]\n\n\ndef parse_args() -> argparse.Namespace:\n    \"\"\"Parse command-line arguments.\"\"\"\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n        description=\"Append one memory event, or atomically rewrite conclusions.md.\",\n    )\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"--write-conclusions\",\n        action=\"store_true\",\n        help=\"Switch into conclusions-write mode (validates and replaces conclusions.md).\",\n    )\n    # Event-append mode (existing behavior).\n    parser.add_argument(\"--event-json\", default=None, help=\"Event payload as JSON object string\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--event-file\", default=None, help=\"Path to event JSON file\")\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"--local-memory-md\",\n        default=\"./memory.md\",\n        help=\"Local workspace memory markdown path\",\n    )\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"--global-memory-md\",\n        default=None,\n        help=\"Global markdown memory path (event mode)\",\n    )\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"--global-memory-jsonl\",\n        default=None,\n        help=\"Global JSONL memory path (event mode)\",\n    )\n    # Conclusions-write mode.\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"--conclusions-md\",\n        default=None,\n        help=\"Path of conclusions.md to overwrite (--write-conclusions mode)\",\n    )\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"--conclusions-history-jsonl\",\n        default=None,\n        help=\"Path of conclusions_history.jsonl to append a record to\",\n    )\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"--conclusions-text\",\n        default=None,\n        help=\"Conclusion paragraph (omit to read from stdin)\",\n    )\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"--cycle-index\",\n        type=int,\n        default=None,\n        help=\"Current cycle index for the history record\",\n    )\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"--worker-index\",\n        type=int,\n        default=None,\n        help=\"Current worker index for the history record\",\n    )\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"--gate-trigger\",\n        default=None,\n        help=\"Gate trigger letter (a|b|c|d) recorded in history\",\n    )\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"--gate-reason\",\n        default=None,\n        help=\"One-sentence reason for the update; for trigger d name the dimension\",\n    )\n    return parser.parse_args()\n\n\ndef _read_conclusions_text(args: argparse.Namespace) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Resolve the candidate paragraph from ``--conclusions-text`` or stdin.\"\"\"\n    if isinstance(args.conclusions_text, str):\n        return args.conclusions_text\n    return sys.stdin.read()\n\n\ndef _validate_conclusions_paragraph(text: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Return the trimmed paragraph or raise ``ValueError`` describing the failure.\"\"\"\n    stripped = text.strip()\n    if not stripped:\n        raise ValueError(\"conclusion paragraph is empty after trimming\")\n    if \"\\n\\n\" in stripped:\n        raise ValueError(\"single paragraph required: text contains a blank line\")\n    word_count = len(stripped.split())\n    if word_count > CONCLUSIONS_WORD_LIMIT:\n        raise ValueError(\n            f\"word count > {CONCLUSIONS_WORD_LIMIT} (got {word_count}); shorten the paragraph\"\n        )\n    return stripped\n\n\ndef _atomic_overwrite(path: Path, text: str) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Atomically replace ``path`` with ``text`` (write to ``.tmp`` then ``os.replace``).\"\"\"\n    path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n    tmp = path.with_suffix(path.suffix + \".tmp\")\n    tmp.write_text(text, encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    os.replace(tmp, path)\n\n\ndef _read_prior_conclusions(path: Path) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Return the prior content of conclusions.md or an empty string if missing.\"\"\"\n    if not path.is_file():\n        return \"\"\n    try:\n        return path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    except OSError:\n        return \"\"\n\n\ndef _validate_conclusions_args(args: argparse.Namespace) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Raise ``ValueError`` if conclusions-mode args are missing or invalid.\"\"\"\n    missing: list[str] = []\n    if not args.conclusions_md:\n        missing.append(\"--conclusions-md\")\n    if not args.conclusions_history_jsonl:\n        missing.append(\"--conclusions-history-jsonl\")\n    if args.cycle_index is None:\n        missing.append(\"--cycle-index\")\n    if args.worker_index is None:\n        missing.append(\"--worker-index\")\n    if not args.gate_trigger:\n        missing.append(\"--gate-trigger\")\n    if not args.gate_reason:\n        missing.append(\"--gate-reason\")\n    if missing:\n        raise ValueError(f\"missing required conclusions-mode flags: {', '.join(missing)}\")\n    trigger = str(args.gate_trigger).strip().lower()\n    if trigger not in GATE_TRIGGER_VALUES:\n        raise ValueError(\n            f\"gate-trigger must be one of {GATE_TRIGGER_VALUES} (got {args.gate_trigger!r})\"\n        )\n\n\ndef _run_write_conclusions(args: argparse.Namespace) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Execute the ``--write-conclusions`` mode.\"\"\"\n    try:\n        _validate_conclusions_args(args)\n    except ValueError as exc:\n        print(f\"append_memory: {exc}\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return 1\n\n    raw_text = _read_conclusions_text(args)\n    try:\n        paragraph = _validate_conclusions_paragraph(raw_text)\n    except ValueError as exc:\n        print(f\"append_memory: rejected conclusions update — {exc}\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return 1\n\n    conclusions_path = Path(args.conclusions_md)\n    history_path = Path(args.conclusions_history_jsonl)\n    prior = _read_prior_conclusions(conclusions_path)\n\n    trigger = str(args.gate_trigger).strip().lower()\n    record = {\n        \"timestamp\": utc_now(),\n        \"cycle_index\": int(args.cycle_index),\n        \"worker_index\": int(args.worker_index),\n        \"gate_trigger\": trigger,\n        \"gate_reason\": str(args.gate_reason).strip(),\n        \"prior\": prior,\n        \"new\": paragraph,\n    }\n    # History append must succeed before the live file changes so a crash\n    # mid-write can never lose the prior version.\n    _append_jsonl(history_path, record)\n    _atomic_overwrite(conclusions_path, paragraph + \"\\n\")\n    return 0\n\n\ndef _run_append_event(args: argparse.Namespace) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Execute the default event-append mode.\"\"\"\n    if not args.global_memory_md or not args.global_memory_jsonl:\n        print(\n            \"append_memory: --global-memory-md and --global-memory-jsonl are required \"\n            \"in event-append mode\",\n            file=sys.stderr,\n        )\n        return 1\n    event_file = Path(args.event_file) if isinstance(args.event_file, str) and args.event_file else None\n    event = _ensure_required_fields(\n        _load_event(event_json=args.event_json, event_file=event_file),\n    )\n\n    local_memory_path = Path(args.local_memory_md)\n    global_memory_md_path = Path(args.global_memory_md)\n    global_memory_jsonl_path = Path(args.global_memory_jsonl)\n\n    _append_text(local_memory_path, _markdown_entry(event), header=LOCAL_MEMORY_HEADERS)\n    _append_text(global_memory_md_path, _markdown_entry(event), header=GLOBAL_MEMORY_HEADER)\n    _append_jsonl(global_memory_jsonl_path, event)\n    return 0\n\n\ndef main() -> int:\n    \"\"\"Run the command-line entry point.\"\"\"\n    args = parse_args()\n    if args.write_conclusions:\n        return _run_write_conclusions(args)\n    return _run_append_event(args)\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    raise SystemExit(main())\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"selected_prompt_family\": \"paper_standalone\",\n  \"recommended_title\": \"Local Residual Transfer for Budgeted Embedding Benchmark Prediction\",\n  \"central_question\": \"How accurately can a sparse historical embedding-score matrix and one to eight queried scores predict a held-out model's sealed benchmark scores under model and heuristic-family holdouts, and how does disrupting historical score structure change that accuracy?\",\n  \"central_thesis\": \"On the pinned MTEB observed-score evaluation, the frozen low-rank predictor with cross-fitted local residual transfer has lower aggregate MAE than the five evaluated baselines and decreasing aggregate error across the tested query budgets, while history-disruption controls expose limits to this advantage without isolating the contribution of residual transfer.\",\n  \"narrative_rationale\": \"The strongest evidence is the completed independent final evaluation, not the search ordering: MAE 0.040807843083862115 versus the minimum aggregate baseline MAE 0.045249969215420516, with budgets 1, 2, 4, 8 and two primary holdout conditions across three seeds. The final evidence also contains evaluator-computed row/checkpoint losses for all five baselines and matched constant-history and column-shift controls. This supports one scoped empirical method study, not a proof of optimal benchmark selection, a residual-component superiority claim, or a generative-LLM claim. Dataset conditions are evidence blocks under one question, not separate discoveries. No downstream_study object is present; final evidence was read independently.\",\n  \"prior_paper_story_status\": \"rejected\",\n  \"edit_framing_decision\": \"Fresh framing, not an edit-mode replacement: no resolved edit-context files, previous_paper directory, prior manuscript, aggregated directory, or extra_tasks directory is available. The rejected option is importing an assumed prior story from memory; there is no prior manuscript to preserve. Compare candidate framings explicitly: a search-success story is rejected as chronological and nonconfirmatory; a broad cross-setting method-family story is rejected because only one pinned embedding dataset is evaluated; a scoped prediction-and-limits story is selected because it matches the final evidence and selected implementation.\",\n  \"staged_input_impact\": [\n    \"The selected implementation is directly available at results/ideas/02_cross_fitted_local_residual_medians/program_1/src/program.py with RESIDUAL_METHOD.md; result.best_correct_score and results/best_program_manifest.json identify this selection. Mechanism description is grounded by inspection, not inferred from the variant name.\",\n    \"The final manifest and evaluator establish a 126-row, 135-task/subset-column final plane from the pinned MTEB snapshot; this narrows the original broad language-model motivation to text-embedding score prediction. These are not 135 independent benchmark suites or the entire public leaderboard.\",\n    \"Final row/checkpoint arrays permit direct budget, holdout and control comparisons; absent outer component ablations prevent claiming residual correction causes the observed gain. Search-only alternatives do not become matched final ablations.\",\n    \"final.qualification.status is completed and final.qualification.outcome is qualified. This is a protocol/evaluation outcome, not proof of novelty, generalization, or performance at every budget. No failed final qualification is present in this run; any later not_qualified outcome must remain disclosed even if metrics are retained.\",\n    \"The references files are present, but available retrieval metadata is not a substitute for reading a paper or support for detailed related-work claims; figure assets are currently absent. This stage plans figures rather than claiming they exist.\"\n  ],\n  \"method_groups\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Low-rank prediction with cross-fitted local residual transfer\",\n      \"role\": \"proposed\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"The frozen studied method: observed-entry ridge ALS, greedy all-column posterior-variance reduction for querying, ridge inference for the new row, and overlap-aware kernel-weighted donor residual medians with history-only tuning and zero-blend fallback. Describe the full evaluated configuration rather than attributing its performance to one unablated component.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Low-rank prediction with random or variance-ranked queries\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Two evaluator-run comparators share the low-rank prediction family but use random or historical-column-variance query ordering. They contextualize the evaluated package, not a clean residual-only or query-only ablation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Linear ridge transfer\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"The evaluator fits ridge transfer from queried scores to all columns using observed historical values with missing centered entries filled by zero; its variance-ranked queries and distinct fitting procedure must be stated. Retain this fixed comparator in main results without inventing an unevaluated matched greedy-ALS baseline.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Column means and observed-score mean\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"The two simple references distinguish a query-free historical prior from copying the new model's queried-score mean across targets. Include both in the main numeric comparison even when the latter is weak; do not omit unfavorable or uninteresting baseline evidence.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Constant-history and independently column-shifted history\",\n      \"role\": \"supporting\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"These are matched data-disruption conditions, not new methods or isolated component ablations. Constant observed values preserve masks; cyclic shifts of each column's value/mask pairs preserve column marginals while disrupting row alignment. They establish a descriptive boundary on the intact-history results, not causal identification or a hidden-adaptivity test.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"results_focus\": [\n    \"Main table: all five baseline methods and the studied method, in a predefined scientific grouping, with overall fixed-target MAE and condition-specific MAE. Report the final RMSE 0.06432104648676243 only for the studied method unless comparator RMSE is actually available; never manufacture baseline RMSE.\",\n    \"Primary aggregate: final.metric.mae = 0.040807843083862115; final.metric.baseline_mae = 0.045249969215420516; final.metric.delta_best_baseline = 0.004442126131558401. The baseline minimum is chosen once after the same overall aggregation, not separately per row, budget or condition. Keep search scores out of this table.\",\n    \"Budget comparison: aggregate MAEs at k=1/2/4/8 are 0.04523334197776794, 0.0422085090615931, 0.03963906108320536, 0.03615046021288207. Plot separate model-holdout and heuristic-family-holdout panels from final.evidence.episodes with the same axes. These are observation-budget comparisons, not optimization trajectories; do not infer that eight queries are sufficient for an unspecified tolerance.\",\n    \"Uncertainty: report seed-level values descriptively and preserve paired component resampling. The supplied gain bounds 0.0021032442082077377 and 0.009199712663056218 are minima across five baseline-specific bounds from 400 component bootstrap draws, using quantiles 0.005/0.995 and RNG 424242 under the evaluator's Bonferroni construction. Describe this construction rather than calling it a conventional single-comparator or seed-independent 95% interval.\",\n    \"Boundary comparison: constant-history MAE is 0.06436711309859705 and independently shifted-history MAE is 0.06856952089296987. Compare these conditions to the matched model-holdout condition, never to a pooled A/B primary mean; show the same fixed baselines alongside them. The shifted condition does not retain an advantage over the column-mean reference in the available arrays. This limitation belongs in the main results and conclusion.\",\n    \"Use sealed, common targets for the primary claim, with uniform targets within row, uniform rows within episode, equal checkpoint weights, equal statistical-seed weights and equal primary-condition weights. All-unqueried losses are a supplementary descriptive view because their evaluated target set changes with the method's selected queries.\",\n    \"No matched final residual-off, unconditional residual, donor-score-only, support-gating, missingness-stratified or cross-leaderboard ablation is available. Training diagnostic code is not outer empirical evidence. Do not fill this gap with search comparisons or invented results.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_plan\": [\n    \"Figure 1, early overview: distinguish historical fitting/calibration from a held-out model's permitted queried scores and sealed targets; show where low-rank prediction and local residual transfer combine.\",\n    \"Figure 2, main accuracy comparison: two aligned panels for model and heuristic-family holdouts, four budget categories and four fixed method series; retain all five comparators in the adjacent main table.\",\n    \"Figure 3, evidence boundary: one grouped comparison of intact, constant and column-shifted history on the matched model-holdout target protocol, with proposed, column-mean and variance-selected low-rank predictors.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_specs\": [\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"method_overview\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Make the sparse score matrix, budgeted queries, historical donor calibration and sealed-target boundary immediately legible without suggesting target access or measured component attribution.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"group_by\": \"information_boundary\",\n      \"metric\": \"none; qualitative mechanism, not numerical evidence\",\n      \"split_by\": \"history-only fit and held-out-model inference\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"paper.sty sets a single 5.5-inch text column and 9-inch text height; full_width is therefore the same physical span as the column, not a two-column figure*. Use two vertically stacked panels, each with at most five labeled objects, so 9-point text remains readable. Place on page 1 or 2, targeting 2.0-2.3 inches of artwork plus a concise caption, about one third of a page. Separate history calibration from inference with a visible boundary; do not shrink to a half-column thumbnail.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 2,\n      \"max_panels_per_row\": 1,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 5,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 2\n    },\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"budget_comparison\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Directly compare sealed-target error versus query budget for the studied method and fixed low-rank/linear references under both held-out conditions.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"line\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"group_by\": \"method_family\",\n      \"metric\": \"fixed sealed-target mean absolute error in normalized score units; lower is better\",\n      \"split_by\": \"model/alias/duplicate holdout and heuristic-family/duplicate holdout\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"Use the full 5.5-inch single-column width for two side-by-side panels, at most two panels per row, one shared external legend, four budget ticks and four series: studied method, random-query low-rank, variance-query low-rank, linear ridge. Target 2.4-2.7 inches of artwork plus caption, roughly 0.4 page on pages 5-6. Use at least 8.5-point rendered labels and identical y limits. Seed standard deviations may be shown as descriptive error bars, not independent-sample confidence intervals; define any uncertainty drawn. If labels collide, stack the panels at the same width rather than shrinking text.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 2,\n      \"max_panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 4,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 4\n    },\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"history_structure_controls\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Expose the intact-history advantage and its limitations under two matched data disruptions without presenting controls as mechanism-isolating ablations.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"scatter\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\ntasks: None\ndownstream: None\nfinal keys: dict_keys(['correct', 'evaluation_evidence', 'evaluator_sha256', 'manifest_path', 'metrics', 'outcome', 'program_tree_sha256', 'results_tree_sha256', 'status', 'task_tree_sha256'])\nqualification: None\nregistry qualification: [('final.qualification.correct', {'evaluation_scope': 'sealed_final', 'id': 'final.qualification.correct', 'manifest_path': 'work/results/r03/codex/20261117_111425/final_evaluation/final_manifest.json', 'program_tree_sha256': 'sha256:8fb5e5478b57efd6c63c99fa308cab26a8e03bc783eeac4e2c0c4642f1051ae1', 'results_tree_sha256': 'sha256:cdf8e0d4df69dbf434b99320296f2bd9384baf39d7efc7a7bfea49bdf3ef8d33', 'type': 'final_qualification', 'value': True}), ('final.qualification.outcome', {'evaluation_scope': 'sealed_final', 'id': 'final.qualification.outcome', 'manifest_path': 'work/results/r03/codex/20261117_111425/final_evaluation/final_manifest.json', 'program_tree_sha256': 'sha256:8fb5e5478b57efd6c63c99fa308cab26a8e03bc783eeac4e2c0c4642f1051ae1', 'results_tree_sha256': 'sha256:cdf8e0d4df69dbf434b99320296f2bd9384baf39d7efc7a7bfea49bdf3ef8d33', 'type': 'final_qualification', 'value': 'qualified'}), ('final.qualification.status', {'evaluation_scope': 'sealed_final', 'id': 'final.qualification.status', 'manifest_path': 'work/results/r03/codex/20261117_111425/final_evaluation/final_manifest.json', 'program_tree_sha256': 'sha256:8fb5e5478b57efd6c63c99fa308cab26a8e03bc783eeac4e2c0c4642f1051ae1', 'results_tree_sha256': 'sha256:cdf8e0d4df69dbf434b99320296f2bd9384baf39d7efc7a7bfea49bdf3ef8d33', 'type': 'final_qualification', 'value': 'completed'})]\npymupdf absent\nmatplotlib absent\njsonschema absent\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
